# Every three-point claim in every document, with the element that made it, the width it spans,
# and the two answers `Claim` requires of it. One row per claim: what would narrow this range, and
# who owns the edge it would have to move.
#
# The grain is the claim, not the layer
#   That is what this file is for. `Claim` is the most reused type in the schema: demands,
#   nameplates, quanta, windows, draws, slacks, shares, factors, coupling strengths and eliminated
#   quantities are all claims. Each lives in the table of the thing it describes, which is the right
#   shape for asking about a demand and the wrong shape for asking about a claim. Their parents are
#   many different tables, across two schemas.
#
# `layer` lets a relation at the claim's grain join back
#   The ancestor axis supplies it at ingest. NULL means the claim is about something between layers
#   (a coupling strength, an elimination quantity, a part factor) rather than about one. Without
#   it, a rule needing both grains has to read the per-table copy and reach one parent of many.
#
# The half of the pair easiest to lose
#   Keyed on a bare document ordinal with no way back to the claim, `narrowsWhen` and `boundOrigin`
#   could be counted but never compared with the width they describe, and both rules that make that
#   comparison would run over `layer.demand_*`: a minority of the claims, and one with no single
#   value among them. One would report vacuous; the other would pass on a corpus containing a
#   violation it cannot reach.
#
# Everything the claim said comes with it
#   Its whole provenance, and the date it holds at, so a relation reading claims reads everything a
#   document attached to one rather than a selection somebody thought worth keeping.
#
# The two columns work together
#   `narrowsWhen` says what would make the range smaller; `boundOrigin` says who owns the edge it
#   would have to move. The same width calls for three different courses of action depending on the
#   answers: renegotiate, measure again, or accept it. Read apart they are two counts; read
#   together they are advice.
-- pm:Claim, with its required pm:narrowsWhen and pm:boundOrigin, joined on the claim.
SELECT c.filing, c.seq, c.owns, c.layer,
       c.low, c.mode, c.high, c.unit,
       c.denominator, c.denominator_kind, c.denominator_absent,
       c.prov_party, c.prov_standing_taxonomy, c.prov_standing_value, c.prov_standing_absent,
       c.prov_entered_by, c.prov_approved_by, c.prov_note, c.as_of,
       c.low = c.high AS is_a_point,
       n.condition    AS narrows_condition,
       n.kind         AS narrows_kind,
       n.absent       AS narrows_absent,
       b.origin,
       b.absent       AS origin_absent,
       n.derivation   AS narrows_derivation,
       b.derivation   AS origin_derivation
FROM pm.claim c
JOIN pm.narrowing    n USING (filing, seq)
JOIN pm.bound_origin b USING (filing, seq)
